### How it works

#### The protocol

- Messaging layer: LayerZero is a set of immutable Endpoint contracts deployed on 160+ chains. An application (an "OApp", or an "OFT" for tokens) calls send() on the source-chain Endpoint. A send library (ULN302 in V2) emits a packet and charges the user a fee in the native gas token.

- Verification: The application chooses its own Decentralized Verifier Networks (DVNs): independent operators who attest that the source event happened. It also chooses how many must agree (X-of-Y required/optional). There are about 71 DVNs (June 2026). Examples include Google Cloud, Fidelity Center for Applied Technology, Deutsche Telekom MMS, Worldpay, PayPal, BitGo, Chainlink CCIP adapter, Nethermind (now operated by Twinstake), Polyhedra, Lagrange and Blockaid's "Bridge Sentinel". Security is per application, not shared. That is the protocol's main design claim and also the cause of the KelpDAO loss (see Risks).

- Execution: An Executor (by default LayerZero Labs) pays destination-chain gas and delivers the message. Anyone can execute manually if the Executor does not.

- Admin powers: The LayerZero Labs multisig owns the Endpoints. It can add chains, append new message libraries and set default configurations. Apps that "pin" their own config are not affected by defaults. After April 2026, defaults were raised to at least 3-of-3 DVNs (V2 done July 9, 2026, with about 0.5% of pathways still pending at that time; V1 done Aug 4, 2026) ([Jul 10](https://layerzero.network/blog/ongoing-security-updates-july-10), [Aug 7, 2026](https://layerzero.network/blog/ongoing-security-updates-3)). On June 4, 2026 LayerZero said it would move "all LayerZero multisigs" from 3/5 to 5/7 in June and to 7/10 "by the end of the summer" ([June 4, 2026](https://layerzero.network/blog/ongoing-security-updates)). Completion was not confirmed in later posts, and the ZRO token's own Ethereum owner Safe still read 3-of-5 on-chain on 2026-11-10 (see Section 8).

- OFT standard: A token issuer deploys one contract per chain and keeps a single unified supply (burn/mint, or lock on the home chain). USDT0, PYUSD, USDe, USDG, WBTC, KRW1, CADC and many gas tokens (TAO, TRX, SEI, XRP, MNT, APT, XPL) use OFT.

- Stargate: A liquidity-pool bridge (V1 and V2, "Hydra" for USDC/USDT/ETH) and the consumer front-end for OFT transfers. Acquired by the LayerZero Foundation in August 2025 (STG holder vote; the offer came from the Foundation). LayerZero says the deal valued Stargate at $120M with an effective cash cost of $25M, because the Stargate treasury held $95M ([The ZRO Token](https://layerzero.network/blog/the-zro-token)). Note: the Aug 11, 2026 pricing post calls Stargate OFT transfers a "LayerZero Labs product", so operational responsibility is shared between the entities. "Bus" batched delivery was discontinued on Oct 1, 2026. All transfers now use "Taxi".

#### The token

- Governance: ZRO is used only for the fee-switch referendum today. A governor contract on Arbitrum (vote #4 governor 0x9006d631fc6fc7d454abedec9157db1ae81b73b9, verified as LzFeeSwitchGovernorMain; an earlier instance with the same code is 0x95E761B6a37D3f99ef998f1530eEE3faB8a16b46) runs a yes/no vote every 6 months. It controls the on/off flag contract LayerzeroFeeSwitch at 0xB9582701a5cB61FDe46EF0eea816D404A8FFc637 (feeSwitch() = false on 2026-11-10; only its governor(), 0x9006…73b9, can call activeFeeSwitch()). The question is: "Turn the protocol fee switch on?" Holders vote with their balance aggregated across chains. Labs and Foundation abstain.

- Fee switch (inactive): If approved, LayerZero would charge a protocol fee of up to the DVN and Executor cost of each message. That would roughly double the per-message cost. The fee would be collected by a Treasury contract on the local chain and burned (or converted to ZRO and burned). The quorum is dynamic: 60% at vote #1, falling toward a 20% floor depending on turnout. Vote #4 needed 30.85%.

- Buybacks (active): Stargate revenue (100% since April 2026, per LayerZero) is used each month to buy ZRO on the open market. The ZRO is sent to the Foundation's buyback wallet (see Section 7). These are not burns.

- No staking, no gas role, no ve-locking today. Planned on Zero: ZRO becomes the gas token and the delegated-proof-of-stake asset, and is used to govern Zones. On ATLAS, trading venues stake ZRO for fee-rebate tiers, and 75% of post-rebate fees go to buy and burn ZRO ([Introducing ATLAS](https://layerzero.network/blog/introducing-atlas)).

- Historical oddity: The V1 protocol allowed paying message fees in ZRO. The V1 take rate has been about 0, so this is negligible (DefiLlama V1 methodology).

### Utility

- Real utility today: (1) Vote in the semi-annual fee-switch referendum. (2) An indirect claim on Stargate revenue through Foundation buybacks, which shrink float but do not burn tokens or pay holders. (3) Collateral and trading asset on CEX and perp venues.

- NOT needed for: Sending LayerZero messages or OFT transfers. Users pay in native gas tokens (ETH, BNB, etc.). DVN and Executor operation also does not require ZRO, and there is no ZRO staking or slashing on the current protocol. Using Stargate or bridging USDT0 does not require it either.

- Planned utility (not live): Zero L1 gas, validator and delegator staking, Zone governance, ATLAS venue-tier staking (up to 1% of supply at the top tier), and ATLAS fee buy-and-burn. All of this depends on Zero and ATLAS launching and getting real volume.

- Verdict: Today ZRO is a governance token plus a small discretionary buyback. It is not a fee token. Most of the investment case rests on planned Zero/ATLAS utility.

### Why people buy and sell it

#### Demand drivers

- Zero/ATLAS narrative: A "last blockchain" L1 claiming 2M TPS per Zone, with day-zero partners DTCC, ICE, Citadel Securities, Tether and Google Cloud, plus ARK (Cathie Wood on the advisory board). The pitch is that tokenized markets, clearing and stablecoin payments run on Zero with ZRO as gas, stake and burn asset. The Nov 4, 2026 pilot with 21X AG ran an on-chain order book (10,000 matches per second on a private Zero instance).

- Interop dominance: The company says LayerZero carries about 70% of cross-chain stablecoin flow and 80–87% of cross-chain volume, has handled more than $300B lifetime and supports 165–170+ chains. These figures are self-reported. New issuers keep choosing OFT: Anchorage Digital (Sept 21, 2026, including Tether's USAT), BDACS KRW1 (Sept 7), Loon CADC (Oct 29), and USDT0 on Stellar (Sept 2).

- "All roads lead to ZRO" value-capture promise: Stargate buybacks, a possible future fee switch, Labs discretionary buys ($10M in Nov 2025), and Zero/ATLAS burns. The company also says there will never be a second token.

- Supply removal: The Foundation bought 50M ZRO (5%) from strategic partners in Sept 2025. a16z bought early investors out ($55M, 3-year lock). Citadel, ARK and Tether bought.

- Liquidity and access: Listed on Binance, Coinbase, Upbit and Bithumb, with deep perp markets (about $69.5M combined OI on Hyperliquid and Binance, 2026-11-10 ~16:09 UTC).

#### Sell pressure

- Investor and contributor unlocks run monthly until about mid-2027: about 12.7M ZRO gross per month to strategic partners, which is about 3.2% of the CoinGecko float, plus contributors.

- Foundation's 183M ZRO unlocks at Zero mainnet. That equals about 46% of the current float.

- STG conversions: STG converts to ZRO at a fixed 0.08634 ZRO per STG until Dec 15, 2026. Converted holders may sell (no data on how many do). The implied market ratio swung around parity on 2026-11-10: 0.0816 at about 15:17 UTC (DefiLlama STG $0.1264 / ZRO $1.548), 0.0865 at about 16:05 UTC (CoinGecko STG $0.1288 / ZRO $1.49) and 0.0891 (DefiLlama STG $0.1322 / ZRO $1.483). STG is illiquid (market cap about $11.9M), so these quotes are noisy.

- Security and trust shocks: The KelpDAO exploit (April 2026), plus the disclosure that a multisig signer used the multisig hardware wallet for a personal trade.

- Weak fee capture: Four fee-switch votes failed. Holder value (buybacks, not distributions) is about 0.6% of market cap a year.

### The protocol/business

#### What it does and for whom

- Interoperability ("Better Money Technology"): Messaging and token standards (OApp, OFT, OVault, lzRead) for asset issuers, chains and DeFi apps. Customers are mainly issuers and institutions: Tether/USDT0 (more than $100B lifetime via LayerZero), PayPal (PYUSD), Paxos (USDG), Ethena (USDe), BitGo/WBTC, Ondo, Centrifuge, Fireblocks, Kraken (kBTC), Anchorage. DeFi: ether.fi, Pendle. Chains: 160–170+, including Ethereum and its L2s, Solana, Aptos, Sui, TON, Tron, Stellar and Canton.

- Stargate: The consumer bridge and OFT front-end. Its three revenue lines are bridging and fast swaps, LZ Assets (deployment, yield and transfer fees for issuers such as USDG0, PYUSD0 and AUSD0) and Hydra liquidity fees.

- LayerZero Labs (private company, Vancouver, BC): runs the default Executor, the Essence gas-abstraction service, the Value Transfer API (basis-point fees), Console subscriptions for issuers, and chain-integration deals. Labs says it "has never had to sell a token to fund itself".

- Zero (announced Feb 10, 2026): an L1 that uses ZK proofs (a16z's Jolt) to separate execution from verification. Its components include QMDB storage, FAFO parallel scheduling and SVID networking. It has multiple "Atomicity Zones", claims up to 2M TPS per Zone and about $0.000001 per transaction, and is EVM-compatible.

- ATLAS (Aug 25, 2026): a "headless exchange" backend on Zero that combines matching, clearing, settlement and risk. Trading venues build the front-end and get 20–65% fee rebates depending on ZRO stake and volume.

#### Where it is popular

Mostly B2B. Value moves mainly along stablecoin routes: USDT0 between Ethereum, Arbitrum, L2s, Tron-adjacent routes, Plasma, Stable, Celo and Stellar. There is also an Asian issuer push (KRW1, KRWQ, Nodit DVN in East Asia) and a non-USD stablecoin push (CADC). Retail exposure comes mostly through Stargate and through wallets and exchanges that integrate OFTs without users seeing LayerZero. LayerZero says about 6M unique wallets had used the protocol by the 2024 airdrop. It is cutting the long tail: off-chain support (DVN/Executor) was wound down on chains with little activity, such as Moonbeam, Canto, Sophon and Bitlayer (Jul–Nov 2026), and testnet support was cut to about 10.

