Discrepancies explained: (1) 14,880,362 (on-chain) vs 14,612,493 (Quant/CoinGecko) differs by ~267,900 QNT; the reason is not documented in any source reachable in this session (likely tokens Quant treats as lost/locked) — not reconciled. (2) Circulating supply differs by ~2.5m QNT between CMC (12.07m) and CoinGecko (14.56m); CMC's figure dates from the original allocation (2.6m company reserve) while the on-chain reserve wallet is now nearly empty (see below), so CoinGecko's ~fully-circulating view is more consistent with chain data.

#### Issuance, allocation, unlocks

- Emission schedule: none. All tokens were minted on 2018-06-25. No scheduled inflation, no staking emissions, no vesting contracts were found. There are no scheduled unlocks. Caveat: unscheduled minting remains technically possible through the crowdsale owner key (see "Minting" above).

- Original allocation (per CoinMarketCap FAQ, which reflects Quant's 2018 materials): ~9.9m sold in ICO (on-chain Crowdsale counters: 8,533,112.51 presale + 1,431,146.53 regular = 9,964,259 QNT), ~2.6m company reserve, ~1.3m founders, ~651k advisers; CMC notes reserve tokens "are unlocked and could be sold or issued at any time".

- On-chain trace of the reserve: wallet 0x1f1e4f22…a126 received 3,584,225.50 QNT from the minting/crowdsale-owner wallet 0xf5e38b…c4bc on 2019-06-08, forwarded 2,559,754.71 QNT to 0xc5aef1ad…b420 the same day (consistent with the ~2.6m "company reserve") and 1,024,460.83 QNT to 0x86c04686…50f6 on 2022-04-04 (which then moved everything to 0x7b4576d0…fcb1 in June 2023). The 0xc5aef1 wallet sent out 526k (2019), 804k (2020), 1,089k (2021), 113k (2022), 42k (2025) QNT and now holds 25,938.84 QNT (balanceOf, 2026-11-10). Attribution of these wallets to Quant is inferred from the mint-time flow, not confirmed by Quant.

- Largest holders (Blockscout, 2026-11-10): #1 is the token contract itself (9.55m, burned). The next 199 largest addresses hold 9.555m QNT (~64% of effective supply; recomputed 2026-11-10 ~16:10 UTC). Of those, 31 addresses tagged as exchanges (Binance, Upbit, Crypto.com, Kraken, Bitpanda, Coinspot…) hold ≥2.25m QNT (lower bound; untagged custody wallets not counted). Largest untagged EOAs: 0x826678ad… 600,000; 0x48e9af81… 599,999; 0x7513ae7e… 454,798 — owners unknown.

### How it works

#### The product (for users)

- Overledger is Quant's multi-DLT platform: one set of credentials and one API surface to read/write across public networks (Ethereum, Polygon, Solana, …), public-permissioned and private-permissioned networks. Self-serve connector families listed in the docs: EVM, Solana, Cosmos, Sui, Hedera, Aptos (Overledger docs, "Overview" and "Connectors", fetched 2026-11-10). Customers are banks and financial institutions that want to issue, move and orchestrate tokenised money/assets without building per-chain integrations.

- Gateway / multi-DLT RPC: the Overledger Gateway authenticates the caller, applies the Overledger Firewall (who may reach which network/method), picks a node from a pool (customer's own nodes, other users' nodes, Quant-managed shared nodes) and forwards the request.

- Fusion Rollup ("Layer 2.5"): an OP-Stack optimistic rollup whose state derives from N connected ledgers at once; transaction data is posted to a single permissioned Besu chain and only state roots to the other chains. Primitives: Deposit, Unify (merge chain-specific versions of the same asset), Trigger (cross-chain contract calls), Withdraw/mint. Docs list Fusion Testnet (chain ID 73073, connected to Ethereum Sepolia, Polygon Amoy, Quant's permissioned Besu testnet) and "Fusion Mainnet — active (production)" but with the chain ID shown as a placeholder (<mainnet-chain-id>) (docs updated 2026-06-26). Quant forked op-geth on GitHub on 2026-10-07.

- Trusted Node Program (Quant post, 2026-09-17): Fusion transactions are processed only by KYC-verified operators in jurisdictions the institution selects (pitched for EU DORA Art. 28 compliance).

- Flow Apps: a workflow/orchestration plane (sessions, steps, webhooks, MCP agent access) with optional x402 payment gates settled via Uniswap Permit2.

- Tokenised Deposits-as-a-Service (TDaaS): Quant's offering that lets commercial banks issue and move tokenised deposits without building infrastructure; Quant is the platform provider for the UK's GBTD and the US TCH initiative (see Business section).

#### Token mechanics

- Governance: none. There is no DAO, no on-chain governance, no voting. Quant is a private company (UK opco → UK holdco → Cayman parent, controlled by a Jersey trust; see Key people).

- Burn / buyback / fee switch: none in the token contract (plain 2018 ERC-20: transfer, approve, mint-by-crowdsale). No buyback or burn programme has been announced in any source reachable in this session.

- Legacy licence model (2018–2025): Quant's historical position (as summarised in the CoinMarketCap FAQ) was that Overledger licences are paid for in fiat, Quant uses that to obtain QNT, and QNT is locked for 12 months; developers of multi-chain apps ("mApps") and gateway operators had to hold/lock QNT. The on-chain implementation was the "Overledger Network Community Treasury" (GitHub repo created 2020-03-31). Its published mainnet contracts — Treasury Proxy 0x2ee1f3535325bc596f616f0591d1a1bc85164775, Factory 0x836fe8f597dc6cf4fb86bd3e86ad724dc4327560, Deposit 0x83e9918ddee45a661a31a63474f864d77b156724, Rulelist 0xff42f979d183c88e930bb07ab88ff1211679814b — have 6, 2, 2 and 2 transactions respectively, all on 2020-06-30 (deployment/setup), have never received a QNT transfer, and hold 0 QNT (Etherscan, 2026-11-10). Because the Factory would create per-user payment-channel/escrow contracts, the decisive check is approvals: there are zero QNT Approval events with the mainnet Factory as spender and zero internal transactions from the Factory (Etherscan getLogs/txlistinternal, 2026-11-10) — i.e. no licensee ever locked QNT through it. The repo was archived on 2026-02-06 with the note that it "has technically been replaced by Quant Fusion". No public address where licence-driven QNT locks can be audited was found.

- New (2026) token roles, per Overledger docs: (a) Staking — API endpoints exist to "Record a QNT stake transaction", get "the QNT staking address for this user" and total staked; the Gateway docs describe staking as "locking QNT to earn rewards and unlock higher capacity tiers"; for nodes on public networks "the amount of QNT staked against the node" is a node-routing factor. But the Staking chapter itself reads "Chapter under construction … will release once the new testnet QNT Staking experience lands in the Quant Connect dashboard" (updated 2026-05-21). The source of staking rewards is not documented. (b) Payments — Flow Apps may price gated actions in QNT, a stablecoin (USDC, USDT) "or any other ERC-20"; QNT is optional, not required.

- Gas: QNT is not documented as the gas token of the Fusion rollup; the docs do not name Fusion's native fee token.

### Utility

| Use | Status (2026-11-10) | Evidence |

| --- | --- | --- |

| Payment for Overledger licences / locked for 12 months | Claimed historically; not verifiable on-chain; published treasury contracts unused since 2020; repo archived Feb 2026 | CMC FAQ; GitHub; Etherscan txlist |

| Staking QNT against public-network nodes (routing priority, capacity tiers, rewards) | API surface documented; staking chapter "under construction", testnet experience pending | Overledger docs (Staking, Gateway, Connectors) |

| Payment token in Flow Apps (x402) | Documented as one option among stablecoins/any ERC-20 | Overledger docs, Flow Apps §6.5 |

| Governance | None | No DAO/governance contract exists |

| Gas / collateral / fee share | None documented | Docs; token contract |

| Use in TCH On-Chain Money Initiative or UK GBTD | Not mentioned in Quant's or TCH's announcements or in Ledger Insights coverage; both run on bank-controlled / permissioned infrastructure and settle in bank deposits / central-bank money | Quant press release 2026-09-24; Ledger Insights 2026-09-24 |

Bottom line: QNT's real, verifiable utility today is minimal; it is mainly an exposure/speculation instrument on Quant's commercial success. Quant's bank customers do not need to hold QNT for anything that is publicly documented.

### Why people buy and sell it

#### Demand drivers

- "Bank/TradFi adoption" narrative: QNT is one of the few liquid tokens tied to a vendor actually selected by systemically important payment operators (TCH, which clears/settles >$2tn/day; UK Finance's GBTD with Barclays, HSBC UK, Lloyds, Monzo, Nationwide, NatWest, Santander). The 2026-09-24 TCH announcement triggered a ~4.5x move within a week.

- Small supply with no emissions: ~14.6–14.9m tokens, no scheduled emissions, ~9.55m permanently stuck in the contract — attractive to "scarcity" narratives; FDV ≈ market cap ($2.64bn vs $2.63bn, CoinGecko 2026-11-10 16:08 UTC). Note: aggregator FDV assumes the 14.61m "max supply"; it does not price the still-live mint key.

- Index/category inclusion: CoinGecko classifies QNT in the "Coinbase 50 Index" category, plus Infrastructure and Real World Assets (RWA).

- Expectation of token utility via Fusion staking/x402 payments and the historic "licence fees buy QNT" story.

- Listings/liquidity: top USD volume venues (CoinGecko tickers, 2026-11-10): GroveX $32.1m, Binance $13.1m, Coinbase $5.5m, BloFin $3.9m, Bitvavo $3.6m, WhiteBIT $3.2m, Bybit $3.0m, Kraken $2.3m (24h).

#### Sell pressure

- No emissions/unlocks, so supply-side pressure comes from existing holders: early ICO buyers, reserve/team-linked wallets (the 2.6m reserve was distributed 2019–2022; 1.02m moved from a mint-linked wallet in 2022–23), and ~2.25m+ QNT already on exchange wallets.

- Profit-taking after event pumps: after the 2026-10-01 peak ($287.38, daily 00:00 UTC point) price fell to $179.11 on 2026-10-25 and $180.48 on 2026-11-10 16:08 UTC; daily volume decayed from $1.49bn (2026-09-28) to ~$40–85m (2026-11-01 to 11-10).

- Latent supply risk: the crowdsale owner key can still mint (≥6.47m QNT headroom, ~44% of current supply); no mint since 2018-06-25 and no outgoing tx from the key since 2019-06-08, but no renouncement either.

- "Value doesn't reach the token" realisation: bank deals are paid in fiat to a private company; holders have no claim on that revenue.

