### Current problems and risks

- Token–business disconnect (core risk): Quant's revenue is fiat, booked in private entities; 99.8% of the UK opco's revenue is intra-group. The publicly documented QNT lock mechanism was never used on-chain and has been archived. Official 2026 communications about TCH/GBTD do not mention QNT. A buyer of QNT is not buying a claim on TCH revenue.

- Hype retracement: −37% from the 2026-10-01 peak ($180.48 vs $287.38, 2026-11-10 16:08 UTC); 30d −25.7%; 24h volume ($63.5m) ~4% of the $1.49bn peak. The move was driven by an announcement whose network goes live only in H1 2027.

- Execution and competition inside TCH: TCH runs a second track (an L2 blockchain) without a named vendor; bank consortia can switch or multi-source vendors. Competing infrastructures: R3, Canton/Digital Asset, Kinexys, Partior, Fnality, Cari, Hazel.

- Small company scale: 44 staff, £11.5m revenue, £7.9m cash at the UK opco (FY2025); R&D spend fell 64% YoY; revenue concentrated in "a small number of client relationships" (director's risk statement).

- Opaque governance/ownership: controlled through a Cayman holding and a Jersey trust; no token holder rights; no transparency report on treasury QNT.

- Live mint key / centralisation of supply: the 2018 crowdsale owner key (EOA 0xf5e38b…c4bc) can still mint ≥6.47m QNT (≈44% of effective supply) and up to ~27.5m by re-enabling the sale (eth_call simulation, 2026-11-10). It has not been used since 2019 and Quant has never announced renouncing it; a key compromise or policy change would be a direct supply shock. Screens that read "max supply 14.61m" do not reflect this.

- Supply data inconsistencies: three different "supply" numbers (45.47m contract, 14.88m on-chain effective, 14.61m official) and two circulating figures (12.07m vs 14.56m) — data-quality risk for screens and index providers.

- Concentration: the 199 largest holders after the token contract ≈ 64% of effective supply (Blockscout, 2026-11-10); several 450k–600k QNT wallets of unknown ownership.

- What the market believes (why it is hyped): that Quant is the leading vendor for interbank tokenised deposits in the two most important markets (UK GBTD live; US TCH selected), that this will eventually drive QNT demand via Fusion staking/payments, and that a small ~14.6m supply (fixed by policy, not by code — see mint-key risk) amplifies any demand. Evidence for the first belief is strong (named institutions, live pilots); evidence for the second (token demand) is currently absent.

