Structure:

- NEAR Foundation is a Swiss Stiftung governed by the NEAR Foundation Council (Illia Polosukhin, Diogo Mónica (chair), Chris Donovan, Bowen Wang, Mert Mumtaz). It funds the ecosystem [42][21].

- Near One builds the protocol.

- Defuse Labs builds NEAR Intents.

- NEAR AI builds the AI stack.

- House of Stake (with House of Stake Foundation) runs token-holder governance.

- Official X accounts: @NEARProtocol [1][8] and @NEARFoundation (the latter not verified this run).

### Recent news (last ~90 days)

- 2026-11-09 — NEAR Intents went live inside Talos's institutional OEMS; desks route orders to Intents like any other venue [26].

- 2026-11-08 — Draft "Sovereign Yield Engine" HSP posted: Intents fees into T-bills and venture investments, with returns used for buybacks or dividends. Draft only [40].

- 2026-11-06 — Protocol version 88 activated on mainnet (block 223,314,737, 12:17 UTC), completing the nearcore 2.14.0 upgrade (released 2026-10-26) [4][13].

- 2026-11-04 — Protocol version 87 activated (block 223,012,337, 11:46 UTC): the 30% developer gas rebate is removed and all gas is now burned (HSP-027) [4][13][14].

- 2026-11-05/06 — HoS Head of Governance (Angela) announced she will leave on 2026-11-13. A NEAR Foundation / HoS continuity statement said 11 proposals were processed in Season #1 [19][20].

- 2026-11-03 — PEARL (Proof-of-Useful-Work AI compute token) listed on near.com, confidential by default [27].

- 2026-11-02 — HSP-029 vote closed (opened 2026-10-19): 3.29M veNEAR For, about 0 Against. Status "Timelock", ending about 2026-11-16 [16].

- 2026-10-23 — All Chain Signatures operations now run inside TEEs [24].

- 2026-10-20 — NEAR Business launched: a confidential multisig treasury on Intents covering 35+ chains [25].

- 2026-10-15 — MPC network: 17 independent operators funded from protocol emissions under HSP-007 (up to 21 nodes) [23].

- 2026-10-14 — Mert Mumtaz (CEO of Helius) joined the NEAR Foundation Council [21].

- 2026-10-12 — NEAR hit its 1-year high of $5.89 (CoinGecko daily) [2].

- 2026-10-09 — A user publicly reported a failed 1Click swap of 575,830 TRX (≈$193K): FAILED status since 2026-09-12, no refund, no support response [38].

- 2026-10-07 — HSP-029 posted by House of Stake on behalf of its author Sal Ternullo (SVRN), following a 2026-09-30 forum discussion on reducing issuance to 1.6% and a "path to a fixed supply" [15][17].

- 2026-09-28 — Bitwise NEAR ETF (NRR) began trading on NYSE Arca; custodian Coinbase Custody; staking target 100% [32].

- 2026-09 — Record Intents month: $4.80B volume and $2.20M NEAR revenue [10]. Over 2026-09-08 → 09-22 NEAR rose 86% ($2.31 → $4.30) while 54.5M NEAR was unstaked [15].

- 2026-09-22 — Tokenized US stocks and ETFs (Ondo) tradable on near.com from 30+ chains [28].

- 2026-09-21 — Top-Level Accounts and Recovery Keys live. HSP-028 made House of Stake registrar of the TLA namespace (vote started 2026-08-30, Succeeded) [16][29].

- 2026-09-03 — nearcore 2.13.4: a CODE_RED security release fixing "critical flaws that could cause node crash/DoS". The previous one was 2.13.2 on 2026-07-27 [44].

- 2026-08-24 — Post-quantum roadmap. ML-DSA signing has been live since Q2 2026; Meteor Wallet supports it, Ledger is "coming soon" [30].

- 2026-08-20 — Illia Polosukhin withdrew the "Sovereign Fund" proposal (posted 2026-08-03) after community pushback on discretionary allocation [18].

### Current problems and risks

- Dilution still dominates value capture. On 30-day data, about 2.69M NEAR is issued against about 0.40M NEAR bought back and about 25k burned. Supply rose 2.48% over the last 12 months [3][4]. The HSP-029 cut is not live and needs a validator-adopted upgrade. Even at 1.6%, issuance would be about 21M NEAR a year.

- Buybacks are discretionary and custodial. Bought-back NEAR (4.23M) sits in a multisig and a Sputnik DAO wallet controlled by NEAR-affiliated parties. There is no burn, no lock contract and no published execution rule. Governance "retains full discretion". When withdrawing his Sovereign Fund proposal, the co-founder wrote that buyback NEAR should never come back into circulation but that the mechanism he had proposed "doesn't guarantee that"; no binding non-recirculation mechanism exists today [11][18].

- Revenue concentration and centralization. Nearly all value capture comes from one product line, Intents. That product is operated by NEAR-affiliated entities (Defuse Labs, near.com, 1Click API), and its largest revenue line is NEAR's own front-end. Intents fees also do not need the NEAR token. If NEAR-controlled entities stopped sweeping revenue into NEAR, token holders would have no on-chain claim [10][11].

- The L1 itself is shrinking. Daily transactions are down 85% year on year (30-day averages: 4.47M → 661k), daily active accounts are down 78% (264k → 57.5k), and stablecoins on NEAR are down 23% ($168.8M → $129.6M) [34][35]. L1 fees are about $157k/month [7].

- Competition and volatility in cross-chain swaps. Relay earned 3x Intents' fees over 30 days ($17.2M vs $5.6M). Intents volume swings with volatility: Oct 2026 was 17% below September, and Apr 2026 ($1.94B) was half of Sep [10][37].

- Operational and compliance risk in Intents. There is a public $193K failed-swap complaint. The complainant quotes NEAR's docs as saying FAILED deposits "are currently handled manually" [38]. A July 2026 forum thread asked to freeze stolen BTC that flowed into NEAR Intents [39]. Privacy features (confidential intents, ZEC routing) raise AML/regulatory exposure. No enforcement action was found in this run.

- Node security: six CODE_RED security releases in 2026 (2.10.4, 2.10.6, 2.10.7 in Jan–Mar; 2.11.1 Apr; 2.13.2 Jul; 2.13.4 Sep) fixed crash/DoS flaws [44]. No chain halt was identified in this run.

- Thin governance: 5.39M veNEAR (0.41% of supply) decides economic policy. HSP-029 was authored by the largest disclosed holder (SVRN, ≈4.2% of supply), and the Head of Governance leaves on 2026-11-13 [15][16][20].

- Validator economics: lower yield after HSP-029 could push out small validators. The author estimates 24 of 405 pools lose self-sufficiency at the target rate [15]. The active validator set already shrank from 424 (early Sep 2026) to 406 (2026-09-22) and 338 (2026-11-10) [6][15].

- Post-hype drawdown: the price is about −40% from the 2026-10-12 peak ($5.89 → $3.56), and NRR's NAV fell 20% over the month to 2026-10-30 [1][2][32]. The narrative mix of AI, privacy, ETF and buybacks has already been partly priced in and partly reversed.

- NEAR AI traction is unproven publicly. No revenue is disclosed. A forum thread on 2026-08-29 asked "Is NEAR AI losing the AI distribution race?" and requested metrics and leadership accountability [48].

What the market believes in (hype drivers): NEAR as the "market of markets", a chain-agnostic settlement layer for cross-chain liquidity, institutions (Talos), tokenized stocks and AI agents. This is backed by real, growing, on-chain-verifiable revenue that is converted into NEAR: Intents revenue went from $28k/month in Aug 2025 to $2.5M in Oct 2026. Other beliefs are confidentiality as a moat (confidential TVL +372% over 90 days), a credible supply-reduction path (halving → rebate removal → 1.6% → "fixed supply" debate), and new regulated demand (NRR ETF, SVRN treasury company) [10][12][15][17][32].

