As of 2026-11-10. Live figures were pulled between 15:27 and 16:15 UTC on 2026-11-10 (Ethereum blocks 26,385,426–26,385,626) unless another date is given; market data was refreshed by the fact-checker at 16:12 UTC. Numbers in [brackets] refer to the Sources list in section 13. "Estimate" marks figures I derived myself.

### 1. Executive summary

ENA is the governance token of Ethena. Ethena issues USDe, a "synthetic dollar" worth about $1. The assets behind USDe are partly crypto held against short perpetual futures (a "delta-neutral" basis trade) and partly stablecoins, lending positions and tokenized real-world assets. Holders who stake USDe receive sUSDe, which earns the protocol's yield. Today nothing reaches ENA holders. Protocol fees ran at about $17.3M over the last 30 days (≈$211M a year annualized). That money goes to sUSDe stakers, partner and exchange rewards, and an Aave leverage incentive program. There are no ENA buybacks, burns or distributions, and Ethena's own filing says ENA holders have "no contractual or programmatic right" to revenue [28]. A fee switch passed a Snapshot vote on 2026-09-02 [17][26]. It starts buybacks only when the 14-day average USDe supply reaches $7.5B. Spot supply is about $4.96B today, so USDe must grow about 51% first. The business is solvent (backing 99.9% of USDe supply excluding, and 101.2% including, the $62.0M Reserve Fund, Ethena API 2026-11-10) but much smaller than before: USDe supply peaked at $14.83B on 2025-10-01 and is down about 67% since. The 14-day average that actually triggers the switch was only about $4.65B (estimate, 2026-10-24 to 2026-11-07), so it must rise about 61%. ENA trades at $0.150, down 35% over 30 days, after rallying from an all-time low of $0.070 (2026-06-30) to about $0.27 (2026-09-28). The one thing to watch is USDe supply, measured as a 14-day average against the $7.5B fee-switch threshold. Team, foundation and ecosystem unlocks of about 189M ENA a month (estimate, ≈$28M a month at the current price) continue until about March 2028.

### 2. What the token is

#### Identity and contracts

| Item | Value | Source / how verified |

| --- | --- | --- |

