### 11. Current problems and risks

- Shrunken business. USDe supply is $4.96B against $14.83B at the October 2025 peak. Ethena itself refers to the "collapse in market-wide open interest on 10th October 2025" and a bear market that compressed crypto basis to its "lowest sustained level" [12]. The 2026 drawdown also included a March–April 2026 contraction from $5.92B to $3.90B that culminated in the late-April rsETH incident (Ethena API low $3.75B on 2026-04-28) and a $235M single-day redemption on 2026-07-10 [21][18].

- No value to ENA yet. 100% of distributable yield goes to stakers, partners and incentives. The fee switch needs about +51% growth from spot supply, or about +61% from the current 14-day average (≈$4.65B, estimate). Even then it starts at a 5% take (≈$18M a year, under 1% of market cap) [17].

- Paying for growth. 47% of USDe fees went to the Aave liquid-leverage incentive program in September 2026 (27% in July) [18]. The staking ratio is down to about 24–25%. Much supply is "rented" through rewards.

- Yield competitiveness. sUSDe at ≈5.1% versus Sky USDS. Blockworks found sUSDe below sUSDS on 243 of 705 backtest days even without a fee switch [17]. Aave's USDe supply APR fell to 0.4% [18].

- Unlock overhang. Investor tranches are fully liquid since 2026-10-05. About 189M ENA a month of team/foundation/ecosystem vesting continues to ~Mar 2028 (estimate). StablecoinX's ~20% stake has lockups whose release dates were not verified [11][28].

- Concentration and centralization. Binance is 60–67% of exchange exposure (12% of total backing at end of Q3) [18]. Mint/redeem is permissioned. The 5/11 Dev Multisig controls contracts through a 24-hour timelock. Governance is advisory, with no general quorum rule and low turnout (88 voters on the fee switch). Foundation token decisions (the buyout price, Foundation/ecosystem releases) are off-chain [28].

- New risk types. Tokenized equities (single upgrade key over bStocks, closed-market pricing [18]), CLO funds (JAAA, STAC), institutional credit (FalconX SPV, Kraken, Maple), and proposed bank-swap exposure (SAFO with BNP Paribas, with a CDS trigger framework [23]).

- Whitelabel weakness. Whitelabel supply fell from a peak above $600M in April 2026 to $66M at Q3-end [21][18]. USDtb supply roughly halved between June and November 2026 [19][29].

- Regulatory. sUSDe not offered in the EU/EEA; no US mint users; Ethena Labs argued to the SEC Crypto Task Force (2025-06-11) that synthetic dollars are not securities [28].

- Positives the market is betting on: backing ratio above 100% throughout, weekly third-party proofs of reserve, and the Reserve Fund at 1.4× its recommended size [18]. Investor overhang resolved; rules-based fee switch; growing distribution (Coinbase, Robinhood Chain, Kraken+, Binance); equity/gold basis could lift protocol yield.

